# Mail Knowledge

## Shape

```
infrastructure/mail/
  mail.module.ts
  mail.service.ts            enqueue(tx, template, to, vars) + deliver(message)
  mail-transport.ts          MailTransport interface + MAIL_TRANSPORT token
  smtp.transport.ts          nodemailer adapter
  mail-templates.ts          template name → zod variables schema + render() to subject/text/html
```

Modules call `MailService.enqueue()` only. Nothing outside `infrastructure/mail` imports nodemailer.

```ts
export interface MailTransport {
  send(message: OutgoingMail): Promise<{ messageId: string }>;
}
export interface OutgoingMail {
  from: string;
  headers: Record<string, string>;
  html: string;
  subject: string;
  text: string;
  to: string;
}
```

## SMTP transport

One nodemailer SMTP transport, configured from the typed config (`10-config.mdc`):

| Env | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `SMTP_HOST` | provider host |
| `SMTP_PORT` | 587 (STARTTLS) or 465 (implicit TLS) |
| `SMTP_SECURE` | `true` only for 465; `false` for 587 (STARTTLS is negotiated) |
| `SMTP_USER` / `SMTP_PASS` | credentials, secrets |
| `MAIL_FROM` | `"Product <no-reply@mail.example.com>"`, a domain you control |

```ts
createTransport({
  auth: { pass: config.smtp.pass, user: config.smtp.user },
  host: config.smtp.host,
  pool: true,
  port: config.smtp.port,
  requireTLS: !config.smtp.secure && config.isProduction,
  secure: config.smtp.secure,
});
```

Provider is a config change, not a code change. turboSMTP is typically `pro.turbo-smtp.com`, port
587 STARTTLS or 465 SSL — verify host, ports and auth mode with the provider's current docs. Amazon
SES SMTP, Postmark, Mailgun, SendGrid and any other SMTP relay plug in the same way. A provider's
HTTP API is only worth adopting for a feature SMTP cannot give (e.g. webhooks tied to message ids);
then it is a second `MailTransport` adapter, not a change to callers.

Dev and e2e: **Mailpit** from docker compose — `SMTP_HOST=localhost`, `SMTP_PORT=1025`,
`SMTP_SECURE=false`, no auth. UI at `http://localhost:8025`, HTTP API at `/v1/messages`.

## Templates

One typed registry; a template cannot be enqueued with the wrong variables:

```ts
export const mailTemplates = {
  "auth.otp": z.object({ code: z.string(), expiresInMinutes: z.number().int() }),
  "auth.password-reset": z.object({ resetUrl: z.string().url() }),
} as const;
export type MailTemplate = keyof typeof mailTemplates;
export type MailVars<T extends MailTemplate> = z.infer<(typeof mailTemplates)[T]>;
```

- Templates are typed TypeScript functions (no template engine for one locale, decision H4): each
  returns `subject`, `text` **and** `html`; every variable goes through `escapeHtml` in the html part.
- The template's zod schema is checked on enqueue and again on delivery (a bad payload is a
  `PermanentJobError` → `dead`).
- Links are built from `config.app.publicUrl`, never from the request `Host` header.
- Inline CSS in the layout; no remote images that track opens unless the AC asks for it.

## Sending — always via the outbox

```ts
await this.db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  const user = await this.users.create(tx, dto);
  await this.mail.enqueue(tx, "auth.otp", user, { code, expiresInMinutes: 10 });
});
```

- `enqueue` validates vars with the registry schema, then writes an `outbox_messages` row of type
  `mail.send` in the **same transaction** as the business write (`20-jobs.mdc`). No commit → no
  mail; commit → mail eventually.
- Locale is the recipient's stored `locale`, captured at enqueue time, not the request's
  `X-Locale` (`13-i18n.mdc`).
- The payload stores template, recipient and vars — not rendered HTML. Rendering happens at
  delivery, so a template fix applies to queued mail. Vars may hold one-time secrets (codes,
  links): the dispatcher replaces the payload with `{}` once the row is `done`.
- Idempotency key per message (`<template>:<entityId>:<purpose>`, unique index). Sent as
  `X-Entity-Ref-ID` header too; the dispatcher never sends the same key twice.
- The request returns without waiting for SMTP. An endpoint whose only job is to send mail returns
  202.

## Delivery, retries, failure

- The outbox dispatcher calls `MailService.deliver()`: render → `transport.send()`.
- Transient failures (connection, 4xx SMTP) → retry with exponential backoff; permanent (5xx
  recipient rejected) → `dead` immediately. Max attempts → `dead`, alert on the dead count.
- Log: outbox id, template, `messageId`, masked recipient (`j***@example.com`), attempt. Never the
  full address, vars, codes, links or body.
- Bounces and complaints: the provider reports them by webhook or a bounce mailbox. Store
  suppressions (`mail_suppressions`: email hash, reason, created_at) and check them in `enqueue`;
  a suppressed address is skipped and logged, not retried.

## Testing

- Unit: bind `MAIL_TRANSPORT` to a `FakeMailTransport` that records messages; assert template,
  locale and vars. Render every template × locale with sample vars in one spec — it catches missing
  variables and broken layouts.
- e2e: real SMTP to Mailpit. Run the dispatcher tick, then read Mailpit's API
  (`GET /v1/search?query=to:<address>`) and assert subject, locale and link. Clear with
  `DELETE /v1/messages` in the reset helper. Use unique addresses per test.

## Deliverability checklist (before production)

- `MAIL_FROM` on a domain you own (a subdomain like `mail.example.com` isolates reputation).
- SPF includes the provider; DKIM keys published and signing verified; DMARC record (`p=none` to
  start, tighten after reports are clean).
- Return-Path / bounce domain aligned per the provider's setup.
- Text part present, no URL shorteners, no all-image mail.

## Don't

- Don't send mail inside a request handler or before the transaction commits.
- Don't call nodemailer or a provider SDK from a module.
- Don't hardcode subjects or bodies in TypeScript.
- Don't log recipients, OTPs, tokens or reset links.
- Don't render with the request locale for a mail the user reads later.
